  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Points $A$ and $B$ are on the graph of $y=x(x-4)$. Given that line $AB$ is parallel to the $x$-axis and $AB=2$, find the coordinates of $A$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$A=(1,-3)$ or $(3,-3)$.\n\nThe horizontal chord is centred on the axis of symmetry $x=2$, so its endpoints lie one unit either side.",
      "worked": "The intercepts are $x=0$ and $x=4$, so the axis of symmetry is\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{0+4}{2}\\\\x&=2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $AB$ is horizontal, its endpoints are equally distant from this axis. With $AB=2$, each endpoint is one unit from it:\n$$\\begin{align*}x_A&=2-1=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2+1=3.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting each value gives\n$$\\begin{align*}y(1)&=1(1-4)=-3,\\\\y(3)&=3(3-4)=-3.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $A=(1,-3)$ or $(3,-3)$. Either endpoint may be labelled $A$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
